# Semantic Release and Workflow Management

> **Related Rules**: [Conventional Commits and PRs](conventional-commits-and-prs.mdc) | [Database Table Creation](database-table-creation.md)

EduHub uses automated semantic versioning with a three-branch workflow and comprehensive release management for consistent, reliable deployments.

## Branch Structure and Workflow

- **`develop`** - Main development branch
  - All feature branches merge here
  - No automated releases (reduces version noise)
  - Automatically deployed to development environment

- **`staging`** - Pre-production testing branch  
  - PRs from `develop` to `staging` for testing
  - No automated releases (focus on production-ready code)
  - Automatically deployed to staging environment
  - Used for final testing before production

- **`production`** - Production releases
  - PRs from `staging` to `production` for final releases
  - Creates final release versions: `0.1.0`, `0.2.0`, etc.
  - Automatically deployed to production environment
  - **Only branch that triggers semantic releases**

## Release Workflow Steps

### 1. Development Phase
- Create feature branch from `develop`
- Make changes using conventional commits
- Create PR to `develop` with title: `chore: merge [feature-branch] into develop`
- Push commits → no automated releases (cleaner version history)
- Automatic deployment to development environment

### 2. Staging Phase
- Create PR from `develop` to `staging`
- PR title: `chore: merge develop into staging for release candidate`
- Review and merge → no automated releases (focus on production readiness)
- Test thoroughly on staging environment
- If issues found, fix on `develop` and create new PR

### 3. Production Phase
- Create PR from `staging` to `production`
- PR title: `chore: promote staging to production release`
- Final review and merge → triggers `0.x.x` production release
- Monitor production deployment
- **Only phase that creates semantic versions**

